1
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:05,690
This picture is about to be scrambled by chaos.

2
00:00:05,630 --> 00:00:10,820
Each step moves every pixel by a simple rule from physics.

3
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:15,050
After a hundred steps, it looks like noise.

4
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:20,220
The rule can be run backwards exactly, at least on paper.

5
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:24,140
So let's run it backwards on a computer.

6
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:31,150
On the left, ordinary floating point. On the right, a version that lives on a grid.

7
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:36,960
Floating point brings back fragments. The grid brings back every pixel.

8
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:40,300
Running chaos backwards.

9
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:46,380
The rule is the kicked rotor, a standard model of chaos.

10
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:51,620
Picture a rod spinning freely, kicked at regular intervals.

11
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:57,570
Each kick changes its speed by an amount that depends on its angle.

12
00:00:57,510 --> 00:01:02,270
Between kicks, the angle drifts forward at that speed.

13
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:09,080
Angle and momentum both wrap around, so every state is a point on a torus.

14
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:16,350
Some starting points circle forever on smooth curves: islands of stability.

15
00:01:16,290 --> 00:01:19,540
Everywhere else is a chaotic sea.

16
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:25,040
Undo the drift, then undo the kick, and you are back where you started.

17
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:30,850
A floating-point number carries about sixteen digits.

18
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:37,490
In the chaotic sea, a tiny error grows about a hundredfold every ten steps.

19
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:44,050
Run fifty steps forward and fifty back, and the first pixels miss home.

20
00:01:43,990 --> 00:01:48,380
After a hundred, only the islands come back.

21
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:53,640
The picture that returns is a map of where the motion is chaotic.

22
00:01:53,580 --> 00:02:01,070
This is Loschmidt's echo: the laws run both ways, but rounding errors do not.

23
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:08,870
Now put the world on a grid of 256 by 256 cells.

24
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:15,240
Round each kick to a whole number of cells, and keep everything else exact.

25
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:20,690
Then a kick slides each column up by a whole number of cells.

26
00:02:20,630 --> 00:02:26,280
A drift slides each row sideways by a whole number of cells.

27
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:33,090
Sliding rows and columns can't make two cells collide, or leave a hole.

28
00:02:33,030 --> 00:02:39,900
So each step just reshuffles the 65,536 cells.

29
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:47,830
Nothing merges and nothing is lost, so running backwards is exact, however chaotic it looks.

30
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:55,750
Here is the machine code: eleven ARM instructions forward, twelve back.

31
00:02:55,690 --> 00:03:02,560
Each point is two bytes, and the kicks are a table of 256 bytes.

32
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:09,600
Byte arithmetic wraps around at 256, which is exactly the torus.

33
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:16,261
In the HOL Light theorem prover, we proved that these exact bytes compute this map,

34
00:03:16,201 --> 00:03:22,211
for every input, every kick table, and any number of points.

35
00:03:22,151 --> 00:03:26,911
We also proved that the second routine undoes the first.

36
00:03:26,851 --> 00:03:34,051
The proof runs on a formal model of the ARM processor from the s2n-bignum project.

37
00:03:33,991 --> 00:03:39,901
It covers the machine code, not the loop that calls it or the pixels you see.

38
00:03:40,951 --> 00:03:46,951
Last time, being symplectic meant a ball could not be squeezed.

39
00:03:46,891 --> 00:03:51,441
In two dimensions, it just means keeping area.

40
00:03:51,381 --> 00:03:56,901
On a grid, keeping area means never losing a single pixel.

41
00:03:56,841 --> 00:04:00,151
Run it back, and the camel comes home.

42
00:04:01,201 --> 00:04:05,131
The interactive version and the proof are linked below.
